1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:00,550
Part two.

2
00:00:00,975 --> 00:00:02,013
The escalation trap.

3
00:00:02,425 --> 00:00:03,588
An escalation trap,

4
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:06,588
Pape explains, is when a strong country attacks

5
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:08,538
a weaker one and wins tactically.

6
00:00:08,962 --> 00:00:10,250
The bombs hit their targets.

7
00:00:10,662 --> 00:00:11,413
Leaders are killed.

8
00:00:11,825 --> 00:00:13,725
And yet the weaker country gains power.

9
00:00:14,138 --> 00:00:14,825
In this war,

10
00:00:14,912 --> 00:00:17,650
he says, Iran quickly tightened its grip on

11
00:00:17,662 --> 00:00:18,737
the Strait of Hormuz,

12
00:00:18,988 --> 00:00:19,550
and with it,

13
00:00:19,938 --> 00:00:21,875
about twenty percent of the world's oil.

14
00:00:22,288 --> 00:00:22,862
Most people,

15
00:00:23,225 --> 00:00:25,725
Pape says, think of strategy as chess.

16
00:00:26,138 --> 00:00:27,050
White takes a pawn,

17
00:00:27,475 --> 00:00:29,525
looks ahead, then loses a bishop.

18
00:00:29,938 --> 00:00:32,362
In chess, the sensible move is to offer

19
00:00:32,375 --> 00:00:32,775
a draw.

20
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:35,025
That's what everyone keeps telling the president:

21
00:00:35,425 --> 00:00:36,125
just take a deal.

22
00:00:36,550 --> 00:00:39,200
But Pape says international politics isn't chess.

23
00:00:39,612 --> 00:00:40,125
It's roulette.

24
00:00:40,538 --> 00:00:41,200
You bet on red.

25
00:00:41,612 --> 00:00:42,462
It comes up black.

26
00:00:42,875 --> 00:00:44,375
A rational player walks away.

27
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:46,062
But the gambler doubles down.

28
00:00:46,488 --> 00:00:47,788
Since red didn't come up,

29
00:00:48,075 --> 00:00:49,150
surely it will next time.

30
00:00:49,562 --> 00:00:50,637
It comes up black again.

31
00:00:51,050 --> 00:00:52,000
Now he's way behind,

32
00:00:52,375 --> 00:00:54,338
so he borrows chips from a bookie and

33
00:00:54,350 --> 00:00:55,250
bets even bigger.

34
00:00:55,675 --> 00:00:57,812
Pape maps each spin onto the war.

35
00:00:58,238 --> 00:00:58,675
Bet one:

36
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:00,225
kill the Supreme Leader,

37
00:01:00,525 --> 00:01:01,438
and Iran will weaken.

38
00:01:01,850 --> 00:01:02,962
It didn't, he says.

39
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:05,538
Bet two: a six week air campaign.

40
00:01:05,950 --> 00:01:07,050
Iran kept the strait.

41
00:01:07,475 --> 00:01:08,862
Bet three: a blockade.

42
00:01:09,288 --> 00:01:10,088
Six months later,

43
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:13,337
Iran hasn't collapsed and gas prices keep rising.

44
00:01:13,750 --> 00:01:14,362
And the chips?

45
00:01:14,775 --> 00:01:16,088
American gas prices,

46
00:01:16,288 --> 00:01:18,700
American lives, American interest rates.

47
00:01:19,125 --> 00:01:21,025
The borrowed chips are the party's support.

48
00:01:21,438 --> 00:01:23,400
And the bookie wants them back on January

49
00:01:23,412 --> 00:01:25,575
third, when the new Congress is seated.

50
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:26,900
So why not just stop?

51
00:01:27,312 --> 00:01:28,650
Because, Pape says,

52
00:01:28,825 --> 00:01:30,975
presidents don't walk away from their legacy.

53
00:01:31,388 --> 00:01:33,912
Lyndon Johnson played the same game in Vietnam,

54
00:01:34,288 --> 00:01:36,775
chasing his losses up the escalation ladder.

55
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:38,850
That pressure of humiliation,

56
00:01:39,238 --> 00:01:41,838
he warns, pushes the loser to keep doubling

57
00:01:41,850 --> 00:01:42,212
down.

58
00:01:42,638 --> 00:01:44,025
Which brings us to the plan on the

59
00:01:44,038 --> 00:01:45,012
president's desk.
